# efs deployment on the fcstor test cluster

**Never use Slurm for efs.** Build/test runs on the dedicated fcstor test
environment, reached via the `efs-test-ssh` wrapper:
`~/.cursor/skills/efs-test-ssh/scripts/efs-ssh.sh <host> '<cmd>'`

**Standing permission — NO approval needed (user, Aug 21 2026; broadened Aug
27 2026):** run scripts against node9901, fstor007, and fcstor003–015
directly, WITHOUT asking the user — including tearing down the whole cluster
and wiping ALL data on fcstor003–006 (fresh mkfs, no rolling restart, no
build-id dance). Run `wipe_cluster.sh` / `clean_cluster.sh` / `run_tests.sh`
straight. These wrapper scripts must run with the sandbox disabled
(`required_permissions: ["all"]`) to reach the ssh-agent + parallel ssh —
that sandbox bypass is pre-authorized for these hosts (a bare
`efs-ssh.sh <host> '<cmd>'` already runs outside the sandbox via the
allowlist). UNLESS the user says differently for a particular run.

**Wipe clients FIRST, then servers, then disks.** A leftover `efs-fuse`
still holds the old table in RAM and will REPORT/flush it onto a fresh
`mkfs` — that is the recurring `adopted cache blob 232MB / chunks=1932296
/ gen=102`. `pkill -x efs-fuse` without `-9` misses D-state daemons.
Use `tests/wipe_cluster.sh` from the login node, or do this by hand:

1. **Every client (fcstor003–015):** `killall -9 efs-fuse` **first**
   (or `pkill -9 -x efs-fuse`), then `timeout 3 fusermount3 -uz /tmp/efs-mount`.
   fusermount-first on a D-state mount hangs SSH forever. Confirm
   `pgrep -x efs-fuse` is empty on **all 13** before touching disks.
   Every remote command needs `EFS_SSH_TIMEOUT` (see efs-remote-timeouts).
2. **Every server:** `pkill -9 -x efsd` (exact name; NEVER `pkill -f`).
3. **Wipe with `edelete`, NEVER `rm -rf`** (rm -rf on millions of chunk
   files takes many minutes; edelete does all 6 paths on 4 nodes in
   seconds):
   `for d in 01..06; do ~/git/ereport/edelete --delete --force /data1/$d/efs; done`
   then `mkdir -p` the dirs again (edelete removes the start dir too).
   Same for any big test-tree cleanup on these nodes.

Do not start `efs-fuse` until `mkfs` has finished. `rm -f /tmp/efs/fuse.log`
before each mount — `rsync --exclude='*.log'` leaves the old log, and a
1-second `cat` of it looks like another 232MB adopt.

## Topology (current)

- 4 servers (`efsd`), port 19810, 6 NVMe paths each
  (`/data1/01/efs` … `/data1/06/efs`), `--quota 36T --direct-io`:
  - node 1 = fcstor003 = 172.16.223.57  (primary/seed — no `--join`)
  - node 2 = fcstor004 = 172.16.223.58  (`--join 172.16.223.57:19810`)
  - node 3 = fcstor005 = 172.16.223.59  (`--join 172.16.223.57:19810`)
  - node 4 = fcstor006 = 172.16.223.60  (`--join 172.16.223.57:19810`)
- 13 clients (`efs-fuse`): fcstor003–015. fcstor003–006 run BOTH efsd + efs-fuse.
- Export `efs-test` (id=1), mounted at `/tmp/efs-mount`.

## Deploy procedure

1. **rsync** repo to node-local scratch on each target (exclude the live mount
   + logs so `--delete` doesn't fight FUSE), run ON the node:
   `rsync -a --delete --exclude='/mnt/' --exclude='*.log' "$HOME/git/efs/" /tmp/efs/`
2. **Build on the node** (never the NFS home, never a login node). Always
   `make clean` ON THE NODE right after the rsync, then a parallel build:
   `cd /tmp/efs && make clean && make -j"$(nproc)" efsd` (servers) /
   `... efs-fuse` (clients). The on-node `make clean` is the safeguard that
   removes any stale `.o` the rsync carried over (see the warning below), so
   the build is always a genuine on-node `-march=native` rebuild.

**NEVER run `make` in the NFS-home workspace (`$HOME/git/efs`) — not even a
"quick compile check".** The login node is Intel (AVX-512); fcstor003–015 are
AMD EPYC Zen2 (**no AVX-512**). A local `make` litters `src/` with `.o` files
built `-march=native` for the login node; the deploy rsync then carries them
to the nodes, where `make` sees them as up-to-date and just relinks → the
binary has AVX-512 (`vmovdqu8 %zmm0`) and **SIGILLs at startup** ("trap
invalid opcode", e.g. in `efsd_install_crash_handlers`). Symptom: fresh efsd
core-dumps before writing any log. Fix: `make clean` in the workspace, then
re-deploy (the rsync `--delete` clears the stale `.o` and forces a real
on-node rebuild). To check compilation, build on a node or use a throwaway
build dir / `make clean` immediately after. (Bit us Aug 27 2026.)
3. **Rolling restart** — one node at a time; verify it rejoins
   (`./efs-mgmt status 172.16.223.57:19810`) before the next.

### Restart one efsd (server)
`pkill -x efsd` — exact name; NEVER `pkill -f "efsd --node-id N"` (the pattern
matches the ssh remote command and kills your own shell). Then:
`cd /tmp/efs && setsid ./efsd --node-id N --addr 172.16.223.5X --port 19810 --storage /data1/01/efs,/data1/02/efs,/data1/03/efs,/data1/04/efs,/data1/05/efs,/data1/06/efs --quota 36T --direct-io [--join 172.16.223.57:19810] >efsd.log 2>&1 </dev/null &`
Use `setsid … </dev/null &` to detach; a bare `nohup … &` can hang the ssh session.

### Restart one efs-fuse (client)
`killall -9 efs-fuse` first, then `timeout 3 fusermount3 -uz /tmp/efs-mount`,
then `rm -f fuse.log` and:
`cd /tmp/efs && setsid ./efs-fuse 172.16.223.57:19810 efs-test /tmp/efs-mount >fuse.log 2>&1 </dev/null &`

## Build-ID gate

`efsd` HELLO requires a matching `EFS_BUILD_ID`. Rolling restart works only
when the ID is unchanged (same commit, `-dirty`). After a NEW commit, restart
ALL servers together — mixed build IDs are rejected.
